'''Aquilinæ''' (ak-wi-lī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Aquila'' + ''-inæ''. Cf. ''aquiline''.] A conventional subfamily of ''Falconidæ'', containing eagles. It has no assignable technical characters. See ''Aquila'', 1.

'''aquiline''' (ak′wi-lin or -līn), ''a.'' [= F. ''aquilin'', < L. ''aquilinus'', pertaining to an eagle, < ''aquila'', an eagle: see ''Aquila''.] '''1.''' Of or pertaining to the eagle.
{{smaller block|<poem>When mortals lived
Of stronger wing, of ''aquiline'' ascent.</poem>
{{right|''Young'', Night Thoughts, ix. 967.}}}}
'''2.''' Resembling an eagle; having the characteristics of an eagle; especially, resembling an eagle’s beak; curving; hooked; prominent.
{{smaller block|<poem>Terribly arched and ''aquiline'' his nose.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', Task, iii.}}
Even before objection was made to his presence in the Board . . . the ''aquiline'' suggestions of Mr. Oakhurst’s mien and countenance not only prematurely fluttered the pigeons, but absolutely occasioned much uneasiness among the fish-hawks. {{right|''Bret Harte'', Argonauts, p. 130.}}}}

'''aquilon†''' (ak′wi-lon), ''n.'' [< F. ''aquilon'', < L. ''aquilo(n-)'', the north wind, Boreas; prob. < ''aquilus'', dark-colored, dun, swarthy (cf. ''Aquila''), with allusion to the dark, stormy weather accompanying the north wind.] The north wind. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Blow, villain, till thy sphered bias cheek
Out-swell the colic of puff’d ''Aquilon''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., iv. 5.}}}}

